1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,667
A race track tilts at its curves.

2
00:00:02,667 --> 00:00:05,620
So does a flyover ramp,
and a cycling track.

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:07,947
A leaning road looks unsafe.

4
00:00:07,947 --> 00:00:09,271
So why build one?

5
00:00:09,721 --> 00:00:12,202
Aarav rides up the flyover near college.

6
00:00:12,202 --> 00:00:15,882
On the curve, the road tilts,
with its outer edge higher.

7
00:00:16,232 --> 00:00:17,636
Why tilt it at all?

8
00:00:17,636 --> 00:00:19,761
And how much tilt is just right?

9
00:00:20,261 --> 00:00:24,080
By the end of this video, you will
find the right angle with one short

10
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:24,980
formula.

11
00:00:25,327 --> 00:00:26,643
So what is banking?

12
00:00:26,993 --> 00:00:30,285
A banked road is a curve
with its outer edge raised.

13
00:00:30,285 --> 00:00:33,137
It tilts toward the
centre by an angle theta.

14
00:00:33,537 --> 00:00:37,948
In episode sixty eight, a flat curve
needed friction to push the car in.

15
00:00:38,348 --> 00:00:42,974
On a banked road, the road's normal
force leans inward, so part of it points

16
00:00:42,974 --> 00:00:44,041
to the centre.

17
00:00:44,641 --> 00:00:48,246
So the road itself can turn
the car, even with no friction.

18
00:00:48,896 --> 00:00:52,756
A one tonne car on a fifty
metre curve, seen from behind.

19
00:00:53,106 --> 00:00:55,195
On a flat road, theta is zero.

20
00:00:55,195 --> 00:00:57,413
The normal force points straight up.

21
00:00:57,413 --> 00:00:58,978
It cannot turn the car.

22
00:00:59,378 --> 00:01:00,761
Now tilt the road.

23
00:01:00,761 --> 00:01:04,295
The normal force tilts too,
by the same angle theta.

24
00:01:04,695 --> 00:01:08,302
Its sideways part, N sine
theta, points to the centre.

25
00:01:08,302 --> 00:01:10,274
That is the centripetal force.

26
00:01:10,674 --> 00:01:14,335
More tilt, more inward push,
so a faster ideal speed.

27
00:01:14,335 --> 00:01:16,115
The dot climbs the graph.

28
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:19,158
Slow-mo replay, at twenty degrees.

29
00:01:19,508 --> 00:01:23,963
At the ideal speed, thirteen point
four metres per second, friction is

30
00:01:23,963 --> 00:01:24,863
zero.

31
00:01:24,921 --> 00:01:28,050
Go faster, and the car
tends to slide up and out.

32
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:29,859
Friction acts down the slope.

33
00:01:30,259 --> 00:01:33,183
Go slower, and it tends
to slide down and in.

34
00:01:33,183 --> 00:01:34,920
Friction acts up the slope.

35
00:01:35,570 --> 00:01:37,914
Now the formula, step by step.

36
00:01:37,914 --> 00:01:39,325
No friction first.

37
00:01:39,675 --> 00:01:40,575
Step one.

38
00:01:40,559 --> 00:01:41,668
Up and down.

39
00:01:41,668 --> 00:01:46,331
The upward part of N, N cos
theta, balances the weight, m g.

40
00:01:47,081 --> 00:01:48,045
Step two.

41
00:01:48,045 --> 00:01:48,945
Sideways.

42
00:01:48,938 --> 00:01:53,778
N sine theta gives the centripetal
force, m v squared over r.

43
00:01:54,528 --> 00:01:55,623
Step three.

44
00:01:55,623 --> 00:01:57,027
Divide the two.

45
00:01:57,027 --> 00:02:00,039
Tan theta equals v squared over r g.

46
00:02:00,039 --> 00:02:03,490
So the ideal speed is root r g tan theta.

47
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:05,140
Step four.

48
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:08,557
Now add friction down the
slope, at its limit, mu N.

49
00:02:09,307 --> 00:02:10,277
Step five.

50
00:02:10,277 --> 00:02:11,302
Solve them.

51
00:02:11,302 --> 00:02:16,729
The top speed is root of r g, times
mu plus tan theta, over one minus mu

52
00:02:16,729 --> 00:02:17,698
tan theta.

53
00:02:18,448 --> 00:02:19,456
Remember this.

54
00:02:19,456 --> 00:02:23,734
On a banked road, the normal force
leans in and helps turn the car.

55
00:02:24,434 --> 00:02:25,968
Now, side by side.

56
00:02:26,318 --> 00:02:29,339
A flat road: only friction turns the car.

57
00:02:29,339 --> 00:02:31,290
Top speed, root mu r g.

58
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:34,142
A banked road: the normal force helps.

59
00:02:34,142 --> 00:02:38,459
Put theta to zero in the big formula,
and you get the flat road back.

60
00:02:39,059 --> 00:02:40,081
Now you try.

61
00:02:40,081 --> 00:02:43,295
A curve is banked for
thirteen metres per second.

62
00:02:43,295 --> 00:02:47,383
On ice, with no grip, a car
goes round at exactly thirteen.

63
00:02:47,383 --> 00:02:49,020
Does it make the turn?

64
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:50,290
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:53,490 --> 00:02:54,390
Yes!

66
00:02:54,053 --> 00:02:58,932
At the ideal speed, N sine theta
alone gives the centripetal force.

67
00:02:58,932 --> 00:03:00,687
No friction is needed.

68
00:03:01,337 --> 00:03:02,237
One more.

69
00:03:02,112 --> 00:03:04,643
A car goes slower than the ideal speed.

70
00:03:04,643 --> 00:03:06,881
Which way does friction act on it?

71
00:03:06,881 --> 00:03:08,043
Pause and think.

72
00:03:11,243 --> 00:03:12,262
Up the slope!

73
00:03:12,262 --> 00:03:16,450
A slow car tends to slide down
the tilt, so friction holds it up.

74
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:18,822
So why tilt the flyover?

75
00:03:18,822 --> 00:03:20,082
Follow the links.

76
00:03:20,432 --> 00:03:21,332
One.

77
00:03:20,982 --> 00:03:24,705
Turning needs an inward
force, m v squared over r.

78
00:03:25,105 --> 00:03:26,005
Two.

79
00:03:25,632 --> 00:03:29,035
On a tilted road, the
normal force leans inward.

80
00:03:29,435 --> 00:03:30,335
Three.

81
00:03:29,984 --> 00:03:33,269
Its sideways part does some,
or all, of the turning.

82
00:03:33,669 --> 00:03:34,569
Four.

83
00:03:34,207 --> 00:03:36,330
So the tyres need less friction.

84
00:03:36,330 --> 00:03:38,453
Less wear, and safer when wet.

85
00:03:39,053 --> 00:03:40,788
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:41,138 --> 00:03:45,892
A flyover curve of radius forty metres
is built for ten metres per second.

87
00:03:45,892 --> 00:03:50,239
Find the bank angle, then the top
speed with mu zero point three.

88
00:03:50,739 --> 00:03:53,233
Tan theta is v squared over r g.

89
00:03:53,233 --> 00:03:56,637
One hundred, over forty
times nine point eight.

90
00:03:56,637 --> 00:03:58,949
About zero point two five five.

91
00:03:59,699 --> 00:04:02,840
So theta is about fourteen
point three degrees.

92
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:04,575
Top speed.

93
00:04:04,575 --> 00:04:09,837
Root of r g, times mu plus tan
theta, over one minus mu tan theta.

94
00:04:10,587 --> 00:04:14,765
That is root of three ninety two,
times zero point six zero one.

95
00:04:14,765 --> 00:04:17,486
About fifteen point
four metres per second.

96
00:04:18,136 --> 00:04:19,602
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:19,952 --> 00:04:24,855
Many students write N equals m g cos
theta, as for a block resting on a

98
00:04:24,855 --> 00:04:25,755
slope.

99
00:04:25,847 --> 00:04:29,406
But at the ideal speed,
N cos theta equals m g.

100
00:04:29,406 --> 00:04:33,241
So N is m g over cos theta,
bigger than the weight.

101
00:04:33,891 --> 00:04:35,398
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:35,398 --> 00:04:38,049
In bank angle and top speed numericals.

103
00:04:38,649 --> 00:04:39,895
Exam level now.

104
00:04:39,895 --> 00:04:44,678
A curve of radius ninety metres is
banked for fifteen metres per second,

105
00:04:44,678 --> 00:04:45,881
and g is ten.

106
00:04:45,881 --> 00:04:48,384
A car takes it at exactly fifteen.

107
00:04:48,384 --> 00:04:51,805
Find the normal force
divided by the car's weight.

108
00:04:51,805 --> 00:04:53,103
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:58,503 --> 00:04:59,906
The answer is A.

110
00:04:59,906 --> 00:05:01,798
Root seventeen over four.

111
00:05:02,298 --> 00:05:04,707
Tan theta is v squared over r g.

112
00:05:04,707 --> 00:05:07,994
Two twenty five over nine
hundred, one quarter.

113
00:05:08,494 --> 00:05:12,205
With no friction, N
equals m g over cos theta.

114
00:05:12,205 --> 00:05:15,232
So N over m g is one over cos theta.

115
00:05:15,732 --> 00:05:18,374
That is root of one plus tan squared.

116
00:05:18,374 --> 00:05:20,310
Root seventeen over sixteen.

117
00:05:20,310 --> 00:05:23,935
Root seventeen over four,
about one point zero three.

118
00:05:24,535 --> 00:05:25,994
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:25,994 --> 00:05:27,494
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:30,694 --> 00:05:33,531
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:33,931 --> 00:05:34,831
One.

122
00:05:34,438 --> 00:05:38,284
Which force turns a car on a
banked road with no friction?

123
00:05:39,184 --> 00:05:40,084
Two.

124
00:05:39,683 --> 00:05:44,168
Why is the normal force on a banked
curve bigger than the car's weight?

125
00:05:45,068 --> 00:05:45,968
Three.

126
00:05:45,669 --> 00:05:48,308
A car goes faster than the ideal speed.

127
00:05:48,308 --> 00:05:50,229
Which way does friction act?

128
00:05:51,329 --> 00:05:55,267
Next time: in a hospital lift,
Diya feels heavier, then lighter.

129
00:05:55,267 --> 00:05:56,731
Does her weight change?

130
00:05:56,731 --> 00:05:59,615
We find out in apparent
weight and weightlessness.
